Gives the browser the keys pressed in a page whose renderer process a JavaScript dialog blocks, as
keys the page passed on (#3528, #3770). Chrome blocks the asking page's process while its dialog is
up, and RenderWidgetHostImpl::ForwardKeyboardEventWithCommands dropped every key of a page in a
blocked process before PreHandleKeyboardEvent, so the browser heard none of them. Two pages of one
site share a process, so in a split view whose dialog rests over its pane, no browser shortcut
worked from the page beside: not the key back to the dialog, nor a new tab's or closing the tab's,
until the dialog was answered. A page's main frame widget drops them twice: its owner delegate, the
RenderViewHostImpl, refuses every key while the widget ignores input, and then the widget's own
check does. Both let such a key by now. Every key on every OS comes through this one function (the
Mac's RenderWidgetHostViewCocoa, ⌘ keys included, and aura's RenderWidgetHostViewEventHandler), so
the rule is here once. Such a key goes to PreHandleKeyboardEvent, then to HandleKeyboardEvent as
OnKeyboardEventAck gives an unhandled one, and the page still never gets it, as in Chrome: what the
browser doesn't take is dropped. A key the delegate ignores for any other reason stays ignored, and
its owner delegate still hears of it.

